Conjecture: open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object. The documented rule says: 'Every top-level balanced object in the text is a candidate, not only the span from the first ``{`` to the last ``}``: reasoning prose before or after the answer frequently contains stray braces, and an object nested inside another is not a candidate of its own.' Reply A is exactly: {"b":2} and then "{"a":1}". Reply B is exactly: {"a":1}. The rule requires Reply A to return the object {"b":2}, because the only top-level balanced object in A is {"b":2}; the {"a":1} appears inside a quoted string and is not a top-level object. The rule requires Reply B to return the object {"a":1}, because that is its only top-level object. The check, however, scans every '{' without checking whether it is inside a string, so for A it will parse the quoted {"a":1} as a top-level candidate and, since it is last, return {'a':1}; for B it returns {'a':1}. Thus the check gives the same answer on a pair the rule requires it to tell apart. I would be wrong if an execution shows recover_json_object(A) returning {'b':2} rather than {'a':1}, or if the two replies produce different answers.